Deviation from the plan: the plan ran the ordering check as
`git log --no-merges --first-parent $(head -1 /tmp/merges)..HEAD`
after the capture. A throwaway-repo test showed that it never fires.
The capture only finds merges after the newest branch commit, so when
a branch commit follows a merge, /tmp/merges is empty and the range
becomes ..HEAD. The check now finds the first merge by itself and runs
before the capture. On non-empty output it says to stop and ask,
because the capture and the `HEAD^2` reset also assume the other
order.

Verified in a scratch repo: a conflicting first merge with an extra
kpi.json change gives "tree differs" after --continue; taking the
merge's file makes the check pass and the loop completes with the
final tree equal to the old tip. The same change in a clean second
merge breaks the loop with "tree differs from <sha>". The ordering
check lists a branch commit added after the merges, and prints
nothing otherwise.

